(6641198, 990251, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First impression: Ahhh!  I can feel the very chaotic nature of that area from here!  So much signage, cars, everything... :-)<br/> <br/>Artistic: A very nice capture of a very busy scene.  With so many signs etc competing for the eye all the time, I can''t help but wonder why there isn''t a huge pileup at every corner.  But as you said in your title, it''s peaceful chaos. The b/w works well and helps to capture the shapes and forms.  In colour it would probably be too overwhelming.<br/> <br/>Technical:  Looks very crisp and not noisy at all, as can be Nikon''s bugaboo.  You did a good job of exposing for the scene, esp with a high ISO, and the light is just enough to illuminate the scene in all its madness without too much glare or punchy dark areas.  Composition is a little drab, shot from lower down or higher up may have given you a more interesting angle. (Just don''t get killed, is all)<br/> <br/>Overall: A good shot of what is clearly par for the course for you in terms of your daily travel.  Keep up the good work and keep shooting!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First impression: Ahhh!  I can feel the very chaotic nature of that area from here!  So much signage, cars, everything... :-) Artistic: A very nice capture of a very busy scene.  With so many signs etc competing for the eye all the time, I can''t help but wonder why there isn''t a huge pileup at every corner.  But as you said in your title, it''s peaceful chaos. The b/w works well and helps to capture the shapes and forms.  In colour it would probably be too overwhelming. Technical:  Looks very crisp and not noisy at all, as can be Nikon''s bugaboo.  You did a good job of exposing for the scene, esp with a high ISO, and the light is just enough to illuminate the scene in all its madness without too much glare or punchy dark areas.  Composition is a little drab, shot from lower down or higher up may have given you a more interesting angle. (Just don''t get killed, is all) Overall: A good shot of what is clearly par for the course for you in terms of your daily travel.  Keep up the good work and keep shooting! Feel free to PM me, Susan', '2012-01-10 18:01:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(6641216, 989636, 80096, '<td valign="top">A beautiful portrait, Brad! Very unlike you but I like what you''ve done here, it shows the versatility of your work. I hope to see more images like this.. the lighting is amazing!</td>', 'A beautiful portrait, Brad! Very unlike you but I like what you''ve done here, it shows the versatility of your work. I hope to see more images like this.. the lighting is amazing!', '2012-01-10 18:21:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(6641784, 990086, 114285, '<td valign="top">Ha, experimental again, wondered whose this was, well done again on a brave shot, which should of rated much higher.</td>', 'Ha, experimental again, wondered whose this was, well done again on a brave shot, which should of rated much higher.', '2012-01-11 01:09:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(6642857, 990591, 28882, '<td valign="top">nicely done.</td>', 'nicely done.', '2012-01-12 00:32:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(6642951, 989213, 89692, '<td valign="top">Wow, that really is stunning!</td>', 'Wow, that really is stunning!', '2012-01-12 06:00:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(6643303, 990152, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First impression: I gave this shot an 8 in voting, and I don''t give them freely.  Good job.<br/> <br/>Artistic:  Nice leading lines and use of thirds, and glad that there isn''t anything dead centre.  I like the wide angle and low POV...sitting or kneeling on the sand, am I right?<br/> <br/>Technical: Good idea to have the horizon straight or you may have drawn some complaints about a tilted horizon. I know it''s basically a landscape shot but I think the addition of an extra element to just left of the mid-centre would have ust given it a little extra.  Someone walking away, or a dog running towards the shore.  Just a thought. Might have been a little bit better with the horizon just a titch lower as it splits the photo almost perfectly in half.  At least at that point, it does :-)<br/> <br/>Overall: A very pleasing landscape, good work.  Keep shooting!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me.<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First impression: I gave this shot an 8 in voting, and I don''t give them freely.  Good job. Artistic:  Nice leading lines and use of thirds, and glad that there isn''t anything dead centre.  I like the wide angle and low POV...sitting or kneeling on the sand, am I right? Technical: Good idea to have the horizon straight or you may have drawn some complaints about a tilted horizon. I know it''s basically a landscape shot but I think the addition of an extra element to just left of the mid-centre would have ust given it a little extra.  Someone walking away, or a dog running towards the shore.  Just a thought. Might have been a little bit better with the horizon just a titch lower as it splits the photo almost perfectly in half.  At least at that point, it does :-) Overall: A very pleasing landscape, good work.  Keep shooting! Feel free to PM me. Susan', '2012-01-12 16:46:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(6643972, 989069, 30214, '<td valign="top">did not see it until now... huge congrats on Top 5 in FS with this lovely shot!</td>', 'did not see it until now... huge congrats on Top 5 in FS with this lovely shot!', '2012-01-13 02:34:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(6644103, 990619, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First impression: Lovely shot of an intriguing piece of architecture, and the pp is subtle enough to see but not overwhelm the photo; it accentuates your subject nicely.  Nice job.<br/> <br/>Artistic: Very dramatic POV and the leading lines angling up to the left, but the arches angling off to the right add to the effect.  The crisp depth of field leads to a sense of being there.  <br/> <br/>Technical: The composition is very appealing with the leading lines and use of thirds.  Presumably the lighting is a mix of natural and interior, in which case b/w is a good choice as colours can look off with more than one light source. The light is a teeny bit close to centred, but I have to have something to pick apart! :-)<br/> <br/>Overall: Great work, I can see by your camera gear that you know how to make the most of what you have.  Wonderful subject and treatment of such, though I agree with the commenter who would like to see the windows in colour.<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me.<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First impression: Lovely shot of an intriguing piece of architecture, and the pp is subtle enough to see but not overwhelm the photo; it accentuates your subject nicely.  Nice job. Artistic: Very dramatic POV and the leading lines angling up to the left, but the arches angling off to the right add to the effect.  The crisp depth of field leads to a sense of being there.   Technical: The composition is very appealing with the leading lines and use of thirds.  Presumably the lighting is a mix of natural and interior, in which case b/w is a good choice as colours can look off with more than one light source. The light is a teeny bit close to centred, but I have to have something to pick apart! :-) Overall: Great work, I can see by your camera gear that you know how to make the most of what you have.  Wonderful subject and treatment of such, though I agree with the commenter who would like to see the windows in colour. Feel free to PM me. Susan', '2012-01-13 08:49:17.000000', NULL, 0),
